    {
      "id": "d9d61d13e9f400d5fbb9572fec626937f5590559",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1999027749,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "A few comments:\n\n1. All the various \"sum total\" statistics (packets/bytes/bits/fields/SUM) should probably have an option / equivalent for dividing by the interval width to show an average over time (as opposed to the \"average over how many times the field appears of AVG), which would also have the advantage of being the same scaling when changing the interval width. (MIN, MAX, AVG would ignore, and also LOAD as it already does something similar.)\n2. The \"divide by the interval width but also multiply by eight, because lots of protocols measure a length in bytes but people are used to comparing bits per second\" feels wrong. We already have a \"Y axis scaling factor\" column which is per graph. I think it makes more sense to have the graph type be a strict average over time, and if people know that the field is bytes but they want bits per sec, they can enter 8 in the \"Y axis scaling factor.\" That keeps this new graph type from being just for \"fields which are bytes but you want to transform it to bits\" - it could be any field type. (I mean, yes, people can also enter `.125` as the Y axis scaling factor for any field that isn't bytes that they want to measure in bits, but that seems more complicated.)",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-07-14T21:19:22.925Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-15T08:56:26.200Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 311988935,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 16199,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1999509561,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "It's constructive, thanks. It's not often clear how to integrate a feature even a minimalistic one in the GUI, I mean in terms of design and keeping the overall logic.\n\n1. This translates into adding a checkbox for each graph or adding more calculations (probably not the way to go because a combobox with many entries is not friendly)\n2. I would agree but at the same time I believe keeping the _throughput_ word in the GUI is somewhat important as Wireshark can't be the only network tool which doesn't quickly give an overview on this indicator. Or at least, if this calculation just added is not kept, the GUI or the User Guide should clearly indicate how to achieve that good old throughput graph by other means.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6797213,
            "username": "eugene.adell",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Eugène Adell",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/6797213/avatar.png?v=1789284959",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/eugene.adell"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-07-15T08:56:26.002Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-15T08:56:26.002Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 311988935,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 16199,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1999807302,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "1. Right. I largely feel that pps, bps, Bps, etc. are _more_ useful than the sum of those over the interval, so if I were starting from the beginning I'd just calculate those as the options, but we've had the totals for a long time. Adding 4 more entries is a lot to the combobox, but those throughputs are useful. (As a side note, after the zero handling was changed, `COUNT FRAMES(Y field)` and `Packets` graph all the same things, though `COUNT FRAMES` makes it slightly easier to switch to a different graph of the same Y field.) A checkbox for each graph that only works on certain graph types is a possibility. I don't love having it only be enabled for certain graph types, but right now the other controls that apply to all graphs tend to set the minimum size of the dialog so there is some room.\n2. I would support keeping it as `THROUGHPUT(Y field)`, which I think makes sense. I just don't really think the \"assume unit is bytes and you want bps so we do an implicit conversion by multiplying by 8\" is particularly obvious, even though I grant that the conversion is a common use case.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-07-15T11:30:13.249Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-15T11:30:13.249Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 311988935,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 16199,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 2003735866,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "1. How could people live for so long without that (I see #18156 looks like a request for this, and there is a _TCP Througput_ in _TCP Stream Graphs_), surely on a large scale people just rely on IPFIX/Netflow but on a smaller scale it's still critical to have this. Back to the topic, the combobox solution is easier and can be implemented quickly. The checkbox enabled for some `Y Axis` values only, probably would be more comfortable for the user. It would also work well with `THROUGHPUT(Y Field)` without the hard-coded *8 but with the `Y Axis Scale=8` instead. The doc should be clear enough for people to understand in that case.\n2. \"Multiply by 8\" is hard-coded and not self-explanatory to the user, agreed. It's not clear also that it's not exactly a throughput based on the packet size but on the real protocol payload (for example a capture made of pure TCP ACK packets only will produce a zero througput by this definition). However I don't have in mind any protocol payload length which is not in Bytes value, but that can be possible for sure. At the end, the checkbox option + `Y Axis Scale=8` with `THROUGHPUT(Y Field)` looks the most flexible solution to me. Let me know, no emergency, I will go back on the _aggregation_ stuff for now.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6797213,
            "username": "eugene.adell",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Eugène Adell",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/6797213/avatar.png?v=1789284959",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/eugene.adell"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-07-17T14:44:52.273Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-17T14:44:52.273Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 311988935,
